WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.050
- Hi everyone, welcome
to Homeroom with Sal.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.840
We have a very exciting show
and very exciting guest today,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.980
Eric Schmidt, but before we
jump into that conversation,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.860
I will give my standard announcements.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.970
First, a reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.870
is a not-for-profit organization

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.940
and we can only exist through donations

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.170
from folks like yourself.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.040
So, if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.180
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.850
I also wanna give a special shout out

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.550
to several organizations

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.730
that stepped up over the last few months.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.020
Many of them have been
supporting us over the years,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.120
but especially when they saw that

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.170
we were running at a deficit pre COVID

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:37.170
and our costs went up because of COVID,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.330
we've had 250% of typical traffic,

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.600
a special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.580
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis
for helping us fill that gap

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.080
but we still need more
help, especially for 2021.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.570
So please think about making a gift.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.100
I also wanna make a reminder announcement

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.290
that there's a version of this
live stream that you can get

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.330
wherever you get your podcast
Homeroom with Sal The podcast.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:06.330
So with that, I am super
excited to introduce our guest,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.870
Eric Schmidt.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.090
Eric there's a lot of
things folks know about you,

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.590
you are a former CEO of Google,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.350
you were an executive chairman of Google,

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.820
you're an advisor to Google,

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.280
you're actually a former
board member of Khan Academy,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.040
you've been a supporter of
Khan Academy over the years,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.363
thank you for joining us.

00:01:25.703 --> 00:01:28.700
- Well, thank you Sal and
you know, when we first met

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.700
and you had this little
operation in Mountain View,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.110
I knew the impact that you
would have would be fantastic

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.030
but the combination of a new set of tools

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.150
and frankly, the pandemic
has accelerated you

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.480
to this sort of new level.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.740
That's really remarkable,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.750
so congratulations to you and the team.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.053
I'm sure it's been intense.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.000
- It has.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.360
Obviously, it's been an
unfortunate situation for the world

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.970
but it's been nice that we
had something to do (laughing)

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.140
something very critical to do so.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:01:59.973
So there's--

00:01:59.973 --> 00:02:02.493
- Astonishing by the
way, I learned one thing,

00:02:02.493 --> 00:02:07.010
I learned many things from
you when I was on your board,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.130
but I don't know if you remember

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:12.020
when you first this
idea of looking at math

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.900
and trying to figure out which

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.620
the minimum number of math questions

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.350
to get the maximum amount
of knowledge transferred

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.760
and I thought that was
a great public service

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.067
to all students by the Khan Academy.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:26.690
And so the fact that you could back solve

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.070
to what math exercises you have to do

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.390
would have saved me an
enormous amount of time

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.000
when I was learning math.(chuckles)

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.740
- Well, you seem like
you've done just fine

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.790
and I'm flattered because back in the day,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.970
I thought all of the learning
was coming towards me.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.540
So I'm glad a little bit went
upstream (loughs) to you.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.777
So there's a ton to talk about

00:02:44.777 --> 00:02:48.030
and I encourage everyone
watching on YouTube and Facebook

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.400
put questions in the message boards,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.200
we have team members who
are gonna surface them

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.140
to Eric and myself, literally
about anything and everything.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.373
But one of the things
where I wanna start is

00:02:57.373 --> 00:03:01.040
on a pretty powerful
project you're working on

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.129
and actually I'm collaborating with you on

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:07.070
called, the RISE Program or
the RISE Scholars program.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:08.900
Tell us what this is

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.370
and why you decided to
get involved in this.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.480
- Well, it's interesting
that I tried to figure out

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.900
when I left Google, what
was really important

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.780
and it turns out the most
important thing was talent.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.490
And I was talking to my various friends

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.690
and I said well, how did you know

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.770
that you were unique in this area?

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.837
That's code for, are
you a nerd in this way,

00:03:28.837 --> 00:03:31.830
and they said that
roughly what happened was

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.720
when they were a teenager,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.820
which is an awkward time for everybody,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.420
there was some kind of teacher
or mentor or experience

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.770
that got them to understand
that they had a special gift.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.068
So then talking to
everybody, we had this idea

00:03:46.068 --> 00:03:50.200
of building an app, which we
ultimately asked you to build

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.420
so thank you for that.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.910
They would try to identify students

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.120
in that sort of 15, 17 age
group with exceptional talent

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.390
and I don't just mean math though,

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.110
I mean any kind of talent,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.628
but they're really off the charts amazing.

00:04:03.628 --> 00:04:07.932
And the idea was that that if we did this,

00:04:07.932 --> 00:04:09.943
what we would do is we would,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.650
figure a way to get a list for such people

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.770
and then working with the Rhodes Trust,

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.850
we would figure out a way to identify,

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.250
starting with a hundred of them.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.120
So you're part of this grand experience.

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.810
- No, I wanna emphasize how
excited I am about this project.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.450
I mean, you're seeing here on the screen,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.210
some of these students who
are able to submit submissions

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.000
and one of the really
powerful things, I mean

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.850
I think what you touch on is,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.550
there's these super young
people around the world

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.350
and you, myself, we
were all probably lucky

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.780
early in our life to have
certain people really believe us

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.300
and certain people support us

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.930
and if we can create a framework

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:52.740
so that they can feel supported,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.900
selfishly speaking, these
are the young people

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.680
who are going to cure the diseases,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.470
who are gonna solve the
major social problems,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.573
who are going to be the
entrepreneurs, the artists

00:05:00.573 --> 00:05:04.120
that that really move society forward.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.650
On your on your title, oh, go ahead, Eric.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.335
- Well, what happened was,

00:05:08.335 --> 00:05:10.230
so we wanted around, we
talked to you and your team,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.480
we talked to some other teams

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.360
and there are groups
that do a pretty good job

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.890
of surfacing talent,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.730
but they are not uniform across the world.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:23.280
So, became clear that there was talent

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.400
that was not being found.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.790
And so the insight was,
it's pretty reasonable

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.930
that that pretty much any teenager

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.240
has access to either
their own mobile phone

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.930
or someone near them has
them pretty much everywhere.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.890
So we had the idea which again,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.500
I think it was really your idea

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.230
of let's have them start by
submitting videos, right?

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.210
Because a video is hard
for them to fake, right?

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.220
and it's a good project
to get them, who are they?

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.240
What do they care about?

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:51.830
What is special about them?

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.493
So that's the grand experiment.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.610
- Yeah, I remember when
we first started talking

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.860
over a year ago and your
your team reached out

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:02.790
and they said, hey, we're interested

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.010
in finding these really
high potential kids

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.020
around the world,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.020
maybe a lot of them are using Khan Academy

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.300
and I said yes, a lot of them very well

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.390
could be using Khan Academy

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.490
and maybe a lot of them
are watching this right now

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.470
but I said, what would
be really cool is if

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:19.290
we could capture your traditional
academic content mastery,

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.123
whatever you wanna call,

00:06:20.123 --> 00:06:22.200
things that traditional
test scores measure

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.170
'cause that's important

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.330
and honestly, Khan Academy
prepares a lot of that.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.290
You have things like SATs,
which were we partner with

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.360
but then there's other things

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:30.450
that traditionally aren't measured,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.650
like your sense of humor, your creativity,

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.520
your entrepreneurship and
we brainstormed together

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.580
and said, let's create this app

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.510
and the app is called hello world

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.450
where we have essentially have challenges.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:41.840
Actually you have a challenge there

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.170
on how someone can change the world.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.530
I put a challenge on there where

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.680
people need to figure out
ways to make me laugh.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.560
And when they submit the video

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.870
they also get to review
other people's videos

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.854
and help them on their journey
to, to grow themselves.

00:06:53.854 --> 00:06:57.980
And what we're hoping is that
not just for the RISE program,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.200
but this could be a new way

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.720
of folks being able to show who they are

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.710
showcase who they are and
get access to opportunities.

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:07.760
You know, one thing that I'm sure

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.840
a lot of folks are
thinking about is, well,

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:10.673
what does this mean?

00:07:10.673 --> 00:07:11.800
Let's say, let's say I run.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.410
I won, I become a rise scholar.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.740
I'm one of that hundred this year

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.773
what what's going to happen to my life?

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.122
- Well, hopefully, the first thing.

00:07:22.122 --> 00:07:24.550
So at some point the
pandemic will be over.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:27.050
And at that point, that cohort will be

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.360
we'll have some kind of
residential program, you know

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.830
three or four weeks together

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.070
which we think will be transformative.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.120
What we know is that each person has

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.560
a specialized set of
problems and opportunities.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.460
So we'll start with trying to make sure

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.070
that they're operating
at their full potential

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:47.620
with career and career and
college counseling and so forth.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.290
If they need a way to get
into a university in the U S

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.480
we'll find a way.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.710
These are people who will
get into wherever they apply

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.960
but we wanna encourage
them to apply wherever

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.330
as part of this, we've made a commitment

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.090
that it's a lifelong program.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.650
So over time we'll provide
mentoring and help.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.060
And we'll also take the older recipients

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.853
and use them to mentor,
mentor the younger ones.

00:08:08.853 --> 00:08:11.270
Because of the way our model works,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.127
we are also gonna start tracking

00:08:13.127 --> 00:08:16.080
whether the signals that your app produces

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.800
ultimately are predictive
of future behavior.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.300
So the key thing is that we're

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.530
we have an idea of what shows excellence

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:26.650
and there's some anecdotal
data, but oversee a decade.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:31.420
We'll be able to say these
cohorts and these characteristics

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:33.700
are the most likely to
predict future success

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.280
with the help we're providing.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.030
So that's really interesting to me.

00:08:38.023 --> 00:08:39.730
- And, and I really wanna emphasize this.

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:42.440
I've been telling, you know,
every young person that I meet

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.220
who I think could, you
know, have a chance of this.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.210
I was like, you have to look
seriously at this program.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:49.570
I think you said it in a very light way,

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.460
but, you know, students who win this,

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.630
they're going to get
access to a lot of mentors,

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.820
a lot of support once again, not just now

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.470
and through college, but
for the rest of their lives.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:01.920
If they have ideas

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.700
on how to help humanity,
they wanna start a business.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.530
They wanna start a not-for-profit

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.840
essentially you're creating an ecosystem

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:11.063
for, to support them.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:14.510
- We are and let's discuss,

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.760
what will the world look
like in 25 or 50 years?

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.740
they're gonna be more people.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.510
It's also gonna be more complicated.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:22.710
Humans are really good

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.730
at building complex governance systems

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.960
political systems, social systems.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.590
So this next generation of
neat leaders are needed now.

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:31.630
And they have to be

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:35.020
up to the task of an even more complicated

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.640
and interconnected world.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:37.920
And I'm excited about that.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.540
I think that my general view is

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.500
the sooner we can turn the current regime

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.036
over to a younger generation who can

00:09:45.036 --> 00:09:46.880
you know, fix the mistakes we've made

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.030
and make some new ones,
I think the better.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.720
- And you, you know, and I
forgot it's my bad, you know

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:55.700
you're the co-founder of Schmidt Futures

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:58.830
and Schmidt Futures is kind of the creator

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.360
of this RISE program.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.520
Explain what Schmidt Futures
hopes to do generally speaking

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.370
and how rise fits in that.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.730
- Well it's become the vehicle to express

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:09.110
the things that I care about,

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.590
which started off with talent.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.990
We're also spending a lot
of time funding science.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:15.480
There's a new generation of scientists.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.730
There always is.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.830
And they are accelerating
discoveries in key aspects

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:23.880
of science, medicine, math,
those sorts of things.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.410
And I'm funding at a very
large level, so forth.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:28.880
I'm getting very interested

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:33.050
in the notion of creating
institutes around new problems.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:35.700
So for example, we've
created a virtual Institute

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.980
that's a code for a funding system

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:40.930
to look at the really
hard science problems

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:42.050
and climate change.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.480
I'm not talking about dealing with it.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.300
I'm talking about why we still
don't understand, for example

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.740
the relationship of sea
ice and the sea around it

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.810
and exactly why it melts the way it does,

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.400
these sorts of things.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.470
There's plenty of science to be done,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:56.870
and I'm trying to fund it.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:01.450
And especially since the
government is now funding R and D

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.660
at the lowest percentage
since Sputnik 1.7%.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:06.840
So literally, what is it?

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:10.500
70 years later, it's the same
percentage of GDP for science.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.030
That's insane, right?

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.280
Nevertheless, that's where we are.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:15.930
So this is an opportunity
for private philanthropy.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.780
I'm also extremely interested
in learning and frankly,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.500
this came out of a, I
don't know if you remember,

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.280
but you had an event at your house

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:26.150
before when we could have events in houses

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.960
where we talked a lot about education,

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:30.960
you were with bill Gates,

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.060
who's a big funder of
Khan Academy as well.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:36.990
And one of the ideas there was
to try to build the database

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.910
of, of information about how people learn.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:42.450
So that's called learning
engineering, and it turns out

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.470
we have lots of opinions
about how people learn

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.230
but we don't actually
have any provable data.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.530
We don't have any AI science around this.

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:50.280
So I'm trying to fund that as well.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.740
- That's a pretty bold goal.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.500
I mean, you know, if I
read between the lines

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:55.690
it's really, you're stepping up

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.930
and you are stepping up in massive ways

00:11:57.930 --> 00:11:59.703
to kind of help fill this gap that,

00:11:59.703 --> 00:12:03.960
that normally is done by
nations and governments.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.670
You know, what, what we're in a

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:09.110
obviously a very unique time
with the political environment

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:12.870
with COVID, with what's going
on, on, you know work and,

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.310
and remote work and all of that.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.500
What, and given your, your org, you know

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.960
Schmidt Futures, what is
your sense of the future

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.680
for looking back five years
from now, 10 years from now

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.950
what do you think is gonna
change for the better

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.240
and what do you think is
gonna change for the worse?

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.130
- Well, I, I don't mean
to say it the obvious

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.740
but when historian looks

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.120
when historians look
at the West performance

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:34.400
with respect to the pandemic,

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.373
they're going to give us an F.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:41.110
Democracy as it's currently
formulated is not serving

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:44.140
the health and the health
and safety of the citizens

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:47.980
of Europe and of South America
and of the United States.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:49.920
There are a few democracies in Asia,

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.480
primarily that have managed
to get through this.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.050
There's a couple in Europe,

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:55.480
but otherwise it's been a disaster.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:57.630
The reason I'm hammering on this

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:00.450
is that the compound negative
impact of the pandemic,

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:04.060
especially on minorities
and the underserved

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.910
is going to be hard for us to catch up.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.350
I thought things were going
pretty good before the pandemic

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:09.240
things were getting better.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:11.430
But if you look at ratios of inequality,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.257
the issues of education and so forth,

00:13:13.257 --> 00:13:16.090
one of the reasons that
Khan Academy is so important

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.680
is there's evidence that
especially students of color,

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.212
people who without, without proper means

00:13:22.212 --> 00:13:25.310
are literally going to miss out
of a whole year of education

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:28.220
because the schools are
closed or partially open.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.290
So we've got to collectively
solve this problem.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.250
The future of our nation is at stake.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.030
Having said that as sort
of the number one concern,

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.280
there's all sorts of reasons to think

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.910
that the, the model of
entrepreneurial discovery,

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:42.480
the kind of creativity that we're doing

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.150
is going to accelerate

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:45.210
for all sorts of reasons.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:48.200
So if we can just get
through this pandemic,

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:49.545
with minimal loss of life

00:13:49.545 --> 00:13:52.350
and get people back to school and to work.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.160
Which somehow the, at least
the Trump administration

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.830
seems to have forgotten
to focus on at the moment.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:13:59.850
And we're waiting now on
that Biden transitioning,

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.900
which is two months away which is insane,

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:03.920
during the middle of a pandemic.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.480
But nevertheless, I think
that's where we are.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:07.333
There's every reason to think

00:14:07.333 --> 00:14:11.640
that we can make great progress
in biology and chemistry

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:16.320
in mental health, in use of tele-health.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.320
I mean, there's an example after example.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.690
I'm the chairman of the
re-imagined commission

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.080
for New York state.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.740
And we've looked a lot at
what the future looks like.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.080
And once you have relatively
broadly available broadband

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.400
it's possible to have a
somewhat more effective

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.330
and somewhat more efficient
learning experience

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.340
on an entertaining experience, et cetera.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.460
I'm not one of these people
who thinks we go back.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:38.900
I think we go forward

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.720
and the new world is much
more hybrid as an aside.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.220
One of the things that
the pandemic has proven

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.330
is that people can be
productive working from home

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.020
if they choose to.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.640
So it looks to me

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:53.250
like we've got some new fundamental
learnings in our society

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.200
which are probably positive
after this terrible experience.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:14:58.753
- And there's a lot in that.

00:14:58.753 --> 00:15:01.633
I mean, I'm just the second
to last thing you mentioned

00:15:01.633 --> 00:15:03.030
that I wanna get to the
last thing you mentioned.

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:06.160
The notion of essentially
access to broadband

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.240
and the digital divide, what's yours?

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.040
And, you've had ties to government

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.510
and probably talking
to them, advising them,

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:16.010
obviously your role at Google.

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.880
What, what do you think are the chances

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.850
that we can finally close
the digital divide at home?

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.060
I mean my back of the envelope,

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.240
it would be one to 2%, of one
of these rounds of stimulus

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.460
would essentially do the job.

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.314
What do you think our odds are?

00:15:29.314 --> 00:15:32.520
- That's why, that's why the
current situation is insane.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.550
The government is being forced

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:35.970
because of economic activity,

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:39.420
come up with a number of
trillion dollar bailouts.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.900
All we need is 50 billion

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.850
a hundred billion to both wire
up the rest of the country

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.300
and fund all the rapid testing,

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.570
fund all the Sentinel systems,

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:53.030
fund all the knowledge
systems to keep us safe.

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:55.460
And yet every time this comes
up, it doesn't get funded.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:56.670
There's something wrong here.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.090
There's somebody, some,
some cognitive problem.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:00.710
But in any case in our New York work,

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.710
we discovered that there
are hundreds of thousands

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.410
of households in New York state

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:08.433
that have, do not have
effective access to broadband.

00:16:08.433 --> 00:16:11.630
Now, you might say there's
plenty of rural people

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:12.900
and the rural people suffer

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.952
because their primary access is cellular.

00:16:14.952 --> 00:16:17.640
So literally on a cell phone

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.580
but there are many, many
underserved communities

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.540
within the cities of our nation.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.760
And it's just, it's just stupid.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.980
It's basically because
the prices are too high.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:27.920
There's no subsidy or there are subsidies,

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:30.050
but the people are not aware of it

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:32.100
or the build out has not
completed and so forth.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.800
And we've got to address that.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:34.850
So as part of our commission,

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:36.410
we're making a set of recommendations

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:40.280
which essentially say, in
order to operate going forward

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:42.500
you have to embrace universal broadband

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.020
and you have to measure it.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:45.583
I'll give you a simple example.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:49.300
Under the FCC rules, you
can count connectivity

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:52.900
based on a single household
within a zip code.

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.010
In other words,

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:57.990
the coverage looks a lot
better than it really is.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.160
And so we proposed example crowdsourcing

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.150
the actual coverage maps.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.430
So we can figure out who
actually has broadband.

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:05.570
And you'll be surprised
where the holes are.

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:07.640
Here, I'm in New York city

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.550
New York city, and you spend
a lot of time in New York.

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:11.970
New York has plenty of holes.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:13.893
It's easy to cover if you want to.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:16.720
- That's fascinating.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:18.150
And what, what's your sense?

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.130
You know, assuming we
are able to cover it.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:21.870
And I, the silver lining

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.910
I've never seen more energy
than we're seeing right now

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:27.110
at hopefully closing the
Jill divide, you know

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:29.390
kind of your, your, your last
point that you had made around

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:31.220
the future of work and that people realize

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.690
that they can be reasonably
productive remote,

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:35.590
you know, given your vantage point is,

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.190
former CEO of, you know, the
techiest of tech companies

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:41.600
that has a lot of infrastructure for,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:43.710
that allows people to work remotely.

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:45.270
What, what do you think is gonna happen

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.493
to work and, and business
travel and all of that?

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.190
- Well, there's a couple
of interesting statistics.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.990
One is that only about
30% of the workforce

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.480
can effectively work from home.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.120
Think about the number of people
who were insecurity guards.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:00.240
They open doors.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.570
There have to be in some kind
of manufacturing facility

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.000
to oversee something.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.240
They're teachers who have to
be in a classroom, et cetera.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:10.250
There's so many jobs

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.780
which are not able to work
from home at the moment.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:14.550
And for those who wish to work from home,

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.460
some of them don't have
environments as we discussed

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.060
where they have appropriate broadband

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.070
or maybe they don't even
have a working environment

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:22.100
where they can do their work

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:24.290
because they live in a
relatively cramped place,

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:25.720
which wasn't a problem but now it is

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:27.060
because they have no space to,

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.330
to focus on what you
and are talking about.

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:31.920
So I think these things will adjust.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.560
And I think the most important thing is

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.020
that we're not gonna go back

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:38.040
to the structured nine
to five in the office.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.920
It's going to be more flexible.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.960
They're gonna be more hybrid models.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:44.630
Also, and I don't mean
to be a downer on this,

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.380
but the pandemic will
go on for a long time.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:48.710
Even if you make the most

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.520
most positive assumptions
about vaccine effectiveness

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.750
and so forth, the rate at
which the vaccine works

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.063
the number of people who will get it

00:18:57.063 --> 00:18:59.420
then the effectiveness of the vaccine

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:00.480
in terms of length of time,

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:02.110
it means that we're gonna be operating

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:03.920
in this strange hybrid mass model

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:06.773
for probably more than a
year from now, maybe longer.

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:10.710
So we need to have a plan to get our

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.060
to get our businesses back to work,

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.630
to get our students educated

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:14.870
to have our mental health address,

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.877
and frankly to have some
